1
00:00:01,510 --> 00:00:04,040
弗里曼：这是科学界 最神秘的谜题之一
(Freeman) It's one of the great mysteries of science --

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,340
如果这个谜题得解
a mystery that if solved

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:10,550
我们就要被迫重新思考 我们在宇宙之中的位置
will force us to rethink our place in the universe.

4
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:16,250
地球上的生命是 独一无二的吗？
Is life on Earth unique?

5
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:19,260
或者，生命在宇宙中传播
Or is it spread throughout the cosmos?

6
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:22,130
如果我们和外星人 共享诸星球
If we share the stars with aliens,

7
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:25,660
我们为什么从未见过它们？
why haven't we heard from them?

8
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:28,530
或者，我们真的是孤独的？
Or are we truly alone?

9
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:37,810
空间，时间，生命本身
Space,time,life itself.

10
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:44,280
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

11
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:47,290
♪穿越虫洞 第1x06集♪
♪Through the Wormhole 1x06♪

12
00:00:47,290 --> 00:00:52,090
我们是孤独的吗？
Are We Alone?

13
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,430
我们都曾经仰望夜空
We've all done it --

14
00:00:55,430 --> 00:00:58,100
望着那
stared into the night sky,

15
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:01,170
缀满明星的苍穹
Ngazed at the vast canopy of stars

16
00:01:01,170 --> 00:01:06,070
想要知道，宇宙深处有什么？
and wondered what or who is out there?

17
00:01:06,070 --> 00:01:09,110
宇宙中还有其它生命吗？
Is there other life in the universe?

18
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:12,610
也许在一个和地球类似 有水有空气的行星上？
Maybe on a planet like ours with water and air?

19
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:16,080
或者在其它类型的 行星和卫星上
Or on some other kind of planet or moon

20
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:18,350
存在另一种外星人生命？
supporting an alien form of life?

21
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:27,060
作为一个在密西西比三角洲地区 长大的小男孩儿
As a young boy growing up in the Mississippi delta,

22
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:31,430
夜间，我和我的家人 坐在门廊上
I'd sit on the porch in the evening with my family,

23
00:01:31,430 --> 00:01:33,730
我们聊东聊西
and we'd talk about things.

24
00:01:33,730 --> 00:01:36,770
阴天时候，夜很黑——
When it was cloudy,it was very dark --

25
00:01:36,770 --> 00:01:39,340
相～～当～～黑
pitch black.

26
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:40,940
我们会认为自己是孤独的
We would think we were all alone,

27
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:46,440
然而有时，有人会从黑暗中出现
and then sometimes people would just appear out of the darkness.

28
00:01:46,450 --> 00:01:47,680
摩根，你看到谁来了？
Who you see coming,Morgan?

29
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:49,050
那是Devon和Travis
It's Devon and Travis.

30
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:50,180
嗨，孩子们！
Hi,boys!

31
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:54,190
我们的宇宙只是幻境？
Could our universe be just as deceiving?

32
00:01:54,190 --> 00:01:57,520
也许会从黑暗的空间中
Might other sentient life-forms just appear

33
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:00,930
浮现出其它的高级生命形式
out of the seeming darkness of space?

34
00:02:00,930 --> 00:02:04,530
宇宙中恒星的数量
There are more stars in the universe

35
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:09,230
要比地球上所有沙滩上 沙粒总和还要多
than all the grains of sand on every beach on Earth,

36
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:13,810
在恒星周围，存在无数的行星
and countless planets orbit those stars.

37
00:02:13,810 --> 00:02:15,570
认为我们是宇宙中的唯一生物
So it would be arrogant to think

38
00:02:15,570 --> 00:02:18,980
这是很傲慢的想法
that we are the only creatures in the cosmos.

39
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:22,410
我相信其它世界存在生命
I think it's easy to imagine life on other worlds,

40
00:02:22,410 --> 00:02:23,920
我可不是孤独的
and I'm not alone.

41
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:30,520
各个专业的科学家 都在研究外星人生命
A whole category of scientists investigate alien life.

42
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:34,090
他们总称为天体生物学家
They're called Astrobiologists.

43
00:02:34,090 --> 00:02:38,130
NASA的Ames研究中心的 Lynn Rothschild
Lynn Rothschild of the NASA Ames Research Center

44
00:02:38,130 --> 00:02:41,970
就是其中一员
is one of them.

45
00:02:41,970 --> 00:02:44,570
我很幸运
I am so lucky that I have a job

46
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:47,570
能够有这样一个 极其令人着迷的工作
that is so incredibly fascinating

47
00:02:47,570 --> 00:02:49,770
因为，对我来说
because,to me,

48
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:52,240
让我不对潜在生命着迷
it's impossible not to be fascinated

49
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:54,110
那是不可能的
with the potential for life.

50
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:59,650
寻找那些怪异的，极端的 与众不同的生物
To me,looking for the bizarre,the extreme,the exceptions,

51
00:02:59,650 --> 00:03:02,090
有些就在地球上
and some of it's here on Earth,

52
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:04,460
但也许有些在其它地方
but there may even be some of it elsewhere,

53
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:07,130
我的意思是，这些东西
and,I mean,that's the sort of thing

54
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:09,660
实在是让人难以自拔
that just absolutely blows your mind.

55
00:03:11,260 --> 00:03:14,100
弗里曼：我们的外星人生物理论
(Freeman) We form theories about alien life

56
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:16,500
是建立在地球生命的第一印象上
by first looking at life on Earth.

57
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:18,900
地球充满了
Our planet is filled

58
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:22,010
极其多样的生物
with an incredible diversity of creatures

59
00:03:22,010 --> 00:03:24,640
它们都是在几十亿年的进化中
uniquely tailored to their environment

60
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:27,750
被生活的环境塑造成独特的形式
by billions of years of evolution.

61
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:33,250
我们所见的 适合生命的普通条件
A normal condition for life,what we see,

62
00:03:33,250 --> 00:03:34,690
适中的温度
is all lovely,

63
00:03:34,690 --> 00:03:37,420
诸如此类
and it's just sort of these medium temperatures and so on,

64
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:41,460
但这只是生命已经适应的 各种环境类型的
but it only describes a very tiny slice

65
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:44,700
非常微小的一部分
of where life can actually survive.

66
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:46,660
我们需要液态水
We need to have liquid water,

67
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:48,870
但是有的生物能够打破界限
but there are organisms that can beat the odds

68
00:03:48,870 --> 00:03:51,100
生活在水沸点以上
and live above the boiling temperature of water

69
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:52,970
或者冰点以下的环境
and below the freezing temperature.

70
00:03:52,970 --> 00:03:55,510
我们不能在酸性太强
We have to be in a certain environment

71
00:03:55,510 --> 00:03:58,180
或碱性太强的环境生存
that's not too acidic and not too basic,

72
00:03:58,180 --> 00:03:59,680
然而有些生物
and yet there are organisms

73
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:03,450
能够生活在极强的酸性条件下
that can live down incredibly acidic conditions.

74
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:09,920
弗里曼： 地球上的生命 能够在极端环境条件下生存
(Freeman) Life on Earth can survive in extreme environments,

75
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:13,790
但是生命能够在 其它世界存活吗？
but can life survive on other worlds?

76
00:04:16,530 --> 00:04:19,530
这是一个宇宙之谜...
It's a mystery set in space...

77
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:21,870
人类几乎没有离开过地球
And that makes it tough to investigate

78
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:24,400
这也使得 寻找外星生命的工作很困难
because the human race has barely left Earth.

79
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:27,970
寻找线索是极其困难的
Searching for clues is incredibly difficult.

80
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:32,510
幸运的是，有时线索会 找上门来
Fortunately,sometimes the clues come to us.

81
00:04:32,510 --> 00:04:35,910
1969年
In 1969,

82
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:41,420
一个220磅重的陨石 落到了澳洲Murchison
a 220-pound meteorite crashed into Murchison,Australia.

83
00:04:47,830 --> 00:04:50,560
Murchison陨石不只是一块
The Murchison meteorite was more

84
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:52,900
在太空游荡的石头
than just a piece of rock floating through space.

85
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:56,830
它是通向外星人生命之门
It was a doorway to alien life.

86
00:04:56,840 --> 00:05:00,540
在加州科学院
A piece of the meteorite is kept here

87
00:05:00,540 --> 00:05:03,840
保存了一块陨石碎片
at the California Academy of Sciences.

88
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:06,980
(Rothschild)令科学家 如此激动的原因是
(Rothschild) The reason this is so exciting to scientists is

89
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:09,610
这块陨石和太阳系一样古老
that it's as old as the solar system,

90
00:05:09,620 --> 00:05:11,580
我们在陨石上发现了
and yet we found that it contains

91
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:13,220
构建生命的基本材料——
the building blocks of life --

92
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:16,590
诸如氨基酸和 其它类似的化学物
things like amino acids and other sorts of chemicals

93
00:05:16,590 --> 00:05:19,860
这些材料组成了我们和 其它地球生物的身体
that we use to make our own bodies and all life on Earth.

94
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:21,290
这说明
So what it shows is

95
00:05:21,290 --> 00:05:24,860
在地球出现以前
that these building blocks were around in our solar system

96
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:27,430
这些基本材料就已经 在我们的太阳系游荡了
before life arose on the Earth.

97
00:05:27,430 --> 00:05:33,810
弗里曼： 生命可能是 通过这样的陨石来到地球的
(Freeman) Life may have come to Earth in a meteorite like this,

98
00:05:33,810 --> 00:05:35,310
像我们这样的生命
and life similar to ours

99
00:05:35,310 --> 00:05:38,440
也许就是以同样的方式 在宇宙中传播的
may be spread through the universe in the same way,

100
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:42,150
陨石就像是种子
as meteorites act like seeds,

101
00:05:42,150 --> 00:05:46,880
向富饶的行星播撒生命的原料
raining down the ingredients for life on fertile planets.

102
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:53,590
这对我们搜寻E.T.的工作 又具有什么意义呢？
So what does this mean for our hunt for E.T.?

103
00:05:53,590 --> 00:05:55,160
首先
For one thing,

104
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:57,730
如果我们所知的 生命的基本原料
if the common elements of life as we know it

105
00:05:57,730 --> 00:06:00,000
是在宇宙间传播的
are spread throughout the universe,

106
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,670
那么其它行星上的生物 也许和地球生物很相似
then life on other planets might be similar to life on Earth.

107
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:07,510
(Rothschild)如果我们看了 很多科幻电影
(Rothschild) If we look at enough Sci-Fi movies,

108
00:06:07,510 --> 00:06:11,310
在其它星球上 有各种怪异和奇妙的生物
there are all sorts of weird and wonderful aliens out there,

109
00:06:11,310 --> 00:06:13,680
但是我们真正感兴趣的
but what we're really interested in

110
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:16,250
是生物体的组成形式
is what these organisms are built from.

111
00:06:16,250 --> 00:06:18,120
如果你研究地球上的生命
And if you look at life on Earth,

112
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:20,920
我们都是炭基化学的产物
we're all built on a form of carbon chemistry

113
00:06:20,920 --> 00:06:22,420
称作有机化学
called organic chemistry,

114
00:06:22,420 --> 00:06:24,690
这就像是生命的语言
and this seems to be the language of life.

115
00:06:24,690 --> 00:06:26,860
放眼恒星之间
If you look out into the interstellar medium,

116
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:29,690
同样的化合物也存在，和地球一样
the same compounds are there as on Earth.

117
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:33,000
组成我们的第二号物质，是水
If you look at what we're made of secondarily,it's water.

118
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
在宇宙中到处都是水
There's water all over the universe.

119
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,540
就我们的生化规则看
So in terms of our biochemistry,

120
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:40,970
我认为，各处的生命
life everywhere I think would be very similar to Earth

121
00:06:40,970 --> 00:06:42,770
应该和 地球生物很相似
in terms of its biochemistry.

122
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:45,280
弗里曼： 但是外星生命
(Freeman) But alien life

123
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:48,780
并不一定和人类外形相似
would not necessarily look like human life.

124
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:51,720
生命不断演化，以适应所处环境
Life evolves to suit its environment.

125
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:53,150
(Rothschild)影响其它行星上
(Rothschild) One of the big things

126
00:06:53,150 --> 00:06:56,520
生物外形的一个重要因素
that would affect what life looks like on another planet,

127
00:06:56,520 --> 00:06:58,820
是重力条件，特别是
particularly if you're starting to look at large life,

128
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:02,160
对于类似我们这种大个儿的 宏观生物
macroscopic life like us,is what the gravity's like.

129
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:06,000
现在，地球上，我们都是在1g 1个重力单位的条件下演化
Now,on Earth,we've all evolved under 1g,1 gravity.

130
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:12,070
但是，举例来说 如果我们在一个2g或5g的星球演进
But if we evolved on a planet with 2g or 5g,for example,

131
00:07:12,070 --> 00:07:14,710
我们可能会有相当粗的腿
we would have to have correspondingly thicker legs.

132
00:07:14,710 --> 00:07:17,410
我们不得不具备更强壮的腿
We would have to have much stronger limbs

133
00:07:17,410 --> 00:07:18,840
以支撑自身的重量
to hold up our weight,

134
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:20,140
也许在某些点
and maybe at some point,

135
00:07:20,150 --> 00:07:22,310
我们会在地上摊成一堆
we'd be pushed so were basically flat on the ground

136
00:07:22,310 --> 00:07:24,680
因为我们根本无法承受任何重量
because we couldn't hold up any weight at all.

137
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:26,320
但是，相反的
But conversely,

138
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:30,150
如果我们在一个 只有二分之一重力的星球上进化
if we had evolved on a planet that,say,had 1/2g,

139
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:34,130
也许我们都会又高又瘦 长得像阿凡达——
maybe we'd be tall and thin and avatar-like --

140
00:07:34,130 --> 00:07:39,700
而不会像我们今天这般模样 你懂的...
you know,creatures and not the way we are today.

141
00:07:39,700 --> 00:07:43,030
重力是我们不太注意的因素
So gravity is something we don't tend to think about,

142
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:46,440
但那的确是塑造生命的条件之一
but that really is one of the things that shapes life.

143
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:50,880
弗里曼：如果天外存在异性
(Freeman) If there are aliens out there,

144
00:07:50,880 --> 00:07:54,380
找到它们会是极其困难的
finding them will be incredibly difficult.

145
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:57,010
天体生物学家们就像是
Astrobiologists are like detectives

146
00:07:57,020 --> 00:07:59,150
不能亲临犯罪现场的侦探
that can't get near the crime scene.

147
00:07:59,150 --> 00:08:01,050
他们被我们的科技水平所限
They're limited by our technology,

148
00:08:01,050 --> 00:08:06,220
仅仅只能向邻近的行星 发射探测器
which,so far,can barely send probes to nearby planets.

149
00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:08,490
这些探测器的结果表明
These probes tell us that Earth harbors

150
00:08:08,490 --> 00:08:10,730
在我们的太阳系中 只有地球
the only currently known sentient life

151
00:08:10,730 --> 00:08:13,200
存在可探知的生命
in our solar system,

152
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:16,730
但这并不是说 没有任何形式的生命
which doesn't mean there isn't any life out there.

153
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:21,040
NASA对在一个甚至多个 我们的邻居星球上
NASA is optimistic about someday finding simple forms of life

154
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:25,080
找到生命很乐观
on one or more of our neighbors.

155
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:28,110
微生物可能生活在
Microbes may live in the Martian soil

156
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:32,350
火星的土壤或金星的云层中
or float in the clouds of Venus.

157
00:08:32,350 --> 00:08:35,250
怪异的生物可能在土星卫星的
Strange creatures could swim in the icy waters

158
00:08:35,250 --> 00:08:37,620
冰水中游弋
of Saturn's moons.

159
00:08:44,430 --> 00:08:47,870
如果我们发现，外星人微生物 在地球外生存
If we find alien microbes outside the Earth

160
00:08:47,870 --> 00:08:51,400
或者甚至是地球微生物在 其它世界欣欣向荣
or even Earth microbes thriving on other worlds,

161
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:53,200
这极大的提高了
it greatly raises the odds

162
00:08:53,210 --> 00:08:56,340
生命在宇宙间传播的可能性
that life is scattered across the universe.

163
00:08:56,340 --> 00:09:01,710
E.T. 也许并不生活在附近
E.T. probably isn't in our neighborhood,

164
00:09:01,710 --> 00:09:05,380
但宇宙是不可想象的大
but the universe is unimaginably large.

165
00:09:05,380 --> 00:09:10,660
他,她或它也许就在那里
He,she,or it may be out there somewhere.

166
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:13,090
在世界范围内
So around the world,

167
00:09:13,090 --> 00:09:15,890
科学侦探们已经发出了
science detectives have sent out an all-points bulletin

168
00:09:15,890 --> 00:09:18,460
针对外星人的江湖追缉令
for extraterrestrial life.

169
00:09:19,730 --> 00:09:21,830
他们监听着星星...
They're listening to the stars...

170
00:09:22,870 --> 00:09:25,840
...甄别可能的安乐窝...
...Staking out possible lairs...

171
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:30,010
汇总草图
Putting together composite sketches...

172
00:09:30,010 --> 00:09:33,910
寻找可能漏过的线索
And looking for clues it may have left behind.

173
00:09:33,910 --> 00:09:36,750
追捕还在继续
The hunt is on.

174
00:09:36,750 --> 00:09:41,590
现在，我们发现了 难以置信的真相
And now we're discovering the incredible truth.

175
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:52,690
我们没有证据证明
We don't have proof

176
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:56,750
我们和其他智慧生物 共享宇宙
that we share the universe with other intelligent species,

177
00:09:56,760 --> 00:10:00,260
但很多科学家相信 智慧生物就在那儿
but many scientists believe that they are out there,

178
00:10:00,260 --> 00:10:01,730
证据，有或无
proof or not.

179
00:10:01,730 --> 00:10:05,460
也许地外生物正在观察着我们
Perhaps extraterrestrials are watching us right now.

180
00:10:06,230 --> 00:10:09,500
也许他们只是向我们发了个信号
Maybe they just sent us a message.

181
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:12,340
也许他们没意识到，我们在这儿
Or maybe they have no idea that we're here,

182
00:10:12,340 --> 00:10:15,870
他们彼此谈话交流
and they're talking among themselves.

183
00:10:15,870 --> 00:10:20,580
如果我们不听 我们就永远不会知道真相
We'll never know the truth unless we listen.

184
00:10:23,150 --> 00:10:26,420
半个世纪以来，SETI计划
For half a century,the SETI Project,

185
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:29,150
也就是地外智慧搜索计划
the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence,

186
00:10:29,150 --> 00:10:31,520
就一直在监听
has done just that.

187
00:10:32,120 --> 00:10:35,890
SETI利用射电望远镜扫描天空
SETI combs the sky with radio telescopes,

188
00:10:35,890 --> 00:10:39,500
希望收到外星来电
hoping to hear alien transmissions.

189
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:42,600
他们试图窃听到E.T.
They're trying to wiretap E.T.,

190
00:10:42,600 --> 00:10:45,140
但他们没有E.T.的电话号码
but they don't have his phone number.

191
00:10:47,970 --> 00:10:51,940
所以，很像CIA搜索 恐怖分子谈话的方式
So,much as the CIA hunts for terrorist chatter,

192
00:10:51,940 --> 00:10:55,480
SETI试图接收太空中的一切
SETI tries to listen to everything out there.

193
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:57,380
这是件令人生畏的工作
It's a daunting task.

194
00:10:57,380 --> 00:11:02,090
对于初学者 我们怎么能分辨
For starters,how can we tell the difference

195
00:11:02,090 --> 00:11:04,890
从恒星发射的自然信号
between a natural signal from the stars

196
00:11:04,890 --> 00:11:10,730
与奇怪的外星人发射的通讯信号 之间的区别？
and an intelligent signal sent by strange alien beings?

197
00:11:10,730 --> 00:11:15,200
Jill Tarter博士是SETI的首席 外星人猎手
Dr. Jill Tarter is SETI's chief alien hunter.

198
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:17,700
[ 电台调整 ]
[ Radio stations tuning ]

199
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:19,700
如果你想倾听宇宙之声
If you'd like to listen to the cosmos,

200
00:11:19,700 --> 00:11:22,540
拿一个这样的老式收音机 一个模拟收音机
take an old-fashioned radio like this,an analog radio,

201
00:11:22,540 --> 00:11:26,740
在频道之间调一调
and tune the radio between stations.

202
00:11:26,750 --> 00:11:27,750
[ 干扰 ]
[ Static ]

203
00:11:27,750 --> 00:11:29,710
你听到那嘶嘶了？
And you hear that hiss?

204
00:11:29,710 --> 00:11:32,020
大约10%	的此类噪声
About 10% of that noise

205
00:11:32,020 --> 00:11:34,520
实际上是来自于宇宙
is actually coming from the cosmos.

206
00:11:34,520 --> 00:11:38,090
这是来自银河系的辐射
That's synchrotron radiation from the Milky Way Galaxy.

207
00:11:38,090 --> 00:11:40,960
[ 频率调制 ]
[ Frequencies modulating ]

208
00:11:40,960 --> 00:11:44,130
弗里曼： SETI搜索这样的宇宙噪声
(Freeman) SETI has combed the cosmic radio dial

209
00:11:44,130 --> 00:11:46,030
已有50年之久了
for 50 years.

210
00:11:46,030 --> 00:11:48,230
大多数日子里，它很安静
Most days,it's quiet out there,

211
00:11:48,230 --> 00:11:52,600
但是一旦有什么激动人心的事情发生 那就要热闹一阵
but once in a great while something exciting happens.

212
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:54,940
[ 频率调制 ]
[ Frequencies modulating ]

213
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:58,540
1977年，一位SETI的天文学家 发现了一个信号
In 1977,a SETI astronomer picked up a signal

214
00:11:58,540 --> 00:12:01,810
信号来自半人马座
from the constellation Cagittarius.

215
00:12:01,810 --> 00:12:04,980
持续了72秒
It lasted 72 seconds.

216
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:06,220
整个过程中
The full duration,

217
00:12:06,220 --> 00:12:08,720
射电望远镜一直瞄着它
the radio telescope was pointed at it.

218
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:11,220
检视数据的过程中
Going through the data,

219
00:12:11,220 --> 00:12:16,030
科学家圈出了这个信号 并写下，"哇噢!"
the scientist circled the signal and wrote,"wow!"

220
00:12:16,030 --> 00:12:18,460
这个信号看起来 像某种经过设计的脉冲
The signal looked like the kind of engineered pulse

221
00:12:18,460 --> 00:12:22,070
正是我们希望 从外星人文明接收到的信号
that we expect will come from an alien civilization.

222
00:12:22,070 --> 00:12:25,670
这个"哇噢!"信号稍纵即逝
The "wow!" Signal was a one-time event.

223
00:12:25,670 --> 00:12:28,470
天文学家们 在那片星空反复寻找
Astronomers have returned to that part of space

224
00:12:28,470 --> 00:12:31,680
希望能够再次捕捉到信号
looking for the signal,hoping for a repeat,

225
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:34,410
但一次次找来的只有失望
but always coming away disappointed.

226
00:12:34,410 --> 00:12:35,710
直到今天
To this day,

227
00:12:35,710 --> 00:12:40,720
仍旧没有解释 不知道发生了什么事
there is still no explanation for what happened.

228
00:12:40,720 --> 00:12:45,390
1997年，在发现"哇噢!"信号20年后
In 1997,20 years after the "wow!" Signal,

229
00:12:45,390 --> 00:12:48,130
SETI在星空中发现了另一个信号
SETI detected another signal from space,

230
00:12:48,130 --> 00:12:51,130
Jill Tarter是目击者
and Jill Tarter was there to witness it.

231
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:56,270
早晨6点钟
At 6:00 in the morning

232
00:12:56,270 --> 00:12:58,900
在西佛吉尼亚的一个观察站
at an observatory in West Virginia,

233
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:01,510
Tarter和她的团队 听到了一个神秘的声音
Tarter and her team heard a mysterious sound

234
00:13:01,510 --> 00:13:05,080
来自鲸鱼座的区域
coming from the Cetus constellation.

235
00:13:05,080 --> 00:13:09,480
Tarter博士：一天清晨，当我们在绿岸 国家射电天文观测站
(Dr. Tarter) The signal showed up early one morning

236
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:13,120
使用140英尺射电望远镜时
at the green bank national radio astronomy observatory

237
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:16,620
这个信号出现了
when we were using the 140-foot telescope there.

238
00:13:16,620 --> 00:13:18,420
我就在望远镜那里
I was actually at the telescope.

239
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:19,790
我们探测到了一个信号
We detected a signal,

240
00:13:19,790 --> 00:13:22,390
那显然是一个人工信号
and it was clearly a manufactured signal.

241
00:13:22,390 --> 00:13:25,160
那不只是仪表盘上的一个记录
It wasn't just one tone on the radio dial.

242
00:13:25,160 --> 00:13:26,460
它像是一个梳子——
It was like a comb --

243
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:30,530
许多不同的信号
many different signals,all narrow,

244
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:34,470
按照频率分离
all separated by the same amount of frequency.

245
00:13:34,470 --> 00:13:36,310
自然母亲可不会这样做
Mother nature wasn't doing that.

246
00:13:36,310 --> 00:13:40,310
弗里曼： 经过了年复一年的收听
(Freeman) After years and years of listening,

247
00:13:40,310 --> 00:13:42,710
年复一年的沉默
years and years of silence,

248
00:13:42,710 --> 00:13:46,280
Tarter想知道,"这就是吗?"
Tarter wondered,"is this it?"

249
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:48,450
 Tarter博士：我兴奋极了
(Dr. Tarter) I was incredibly excited,

250
00:13:48,450 --> 00:13:52,160
我计划在8点钟的时候
and I was scheduled to finish up my observing shift

251
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:54,190
结束我在西佛吉尼亚的观测工作
in West Virginia at 8:00

252
00:13:54,190 --> 00:13:56,860
搭乘飞机回家去
and get on a plane and come home.

253
00:13:56,860 --> 00:13:59,160
当早晨6点，信号开始时
When this started at 6:00 in the morning,

254
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:01,930
我当然不想坐什么飞机
I certainly wasn't gonna get on any plane

255
00:14:01,930 --> 00:14:03,800
更不会把信号放在脑后
and leave the signal behind.

256
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:07,340
弗里曼：Tarter开始了一系列测试
(Freeman) Tarter initiated a series of tests

257
00:14:07,340 --> 00:14:08,770
核实信号
to verify the signal

258
00:14:08,770 --> 00:14:15,610
确定它是天然的，人工的 还是来自外星人的
and find out if it was natural,man-made,or alien-made.

259
00:14:15,610 --> 00:14:19,220
Tarter博士：我们把望远镜从 指向的星球移开
(Dr. Tarter) We moved the telescope off the star we were pointing on,

260
00:14:19,220 --> 00:14:21,150
信号消失了
the signal went away.

261
00:14:21,150 --> 00:14:25,090
我们又把天线瞄准这个星球 信号再次出现
We moved it back on the star,the signal was there.

262
00:14:25,090 --> 00:14:30,860
我想，"哦，我想知道 我们其他的观察人员
I thought,"oh,I wonder if any of our other observations

263
00:14:30,860 --> 00:14:33,060
是否观测到同样的信号?"
have seen that same kind of signal?"

264
00:14:33,060 --> 00:14:37,270
但是，当我看到输出的时候 我极其兴奋
But I was so excited that when I looked at the output,

265
00:14:37,270 --> 00:14:43,140
我不明白我所看的 究竟意味着什么
I didn't see that exactly what I was looking for was there.

266
00:14:43,140 --> 00:14:46,240
以前，我们曾经有机会
We had,at previous occasions,

267
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:49,610
探测到这种频率空间的信号
detected this signal with this frequency spacing.

268
00:14:49,610 --> 00:14:53,250
看上去，这个信号不像是
It turned out that it was a signal that was coming

269
00:14:53,250 --> 00:14:55,920
来自我们所观察的方向
not from the direction that we were looking in,

270
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:59,190
更像是来自我们的侧脑叶
but a signal that was coming in our side lobes.

271
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:04,860
弗里曼：Tarter和她的同事犯了个错
(Freeman) Tarter and her colleagues had made a mistake.

272
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:08,030
他们探测到的信号不是外星人
The signal they detected was not alien.

273
00:15:08,030 --> 00:15:11,670
它来自于一个围绕太阳运行的 名为SOHO的
It came from a man-made space probe orbiting the sun

274
00:15:11,670 --> 00:15:13,570
人造探测器
called Soho.

275
00:15:15,270 --> 00:15:18,140
SETI知道，如果要在宇宙中交流
SETI learned that to communicate in the cosmos,

276
00:15:18,140 --> 00:15:20,880
需要更好的科技
it needed better technology.

277
00:15:20,880 --> 00:15:24,050
现在，他们正如愿以偿
And now they're getting it.

278
00:15:24,050 --> 00:15:26,120
在北加州
In Northern California,

279
00:15:26,120 --> 00:15:30,320
SETI正在建造几百个 用于监听ET的新设备
SETI is building hundreds of new devices to wiretap E.T.

280
00:15:32,520 --> 00:15:35,290
Tarter博士：我们在北加州
(Dr. Tarter) We're here in Northern California

281
00:15:35,290 --> 00:15:38,230
Hat Creek射电观察站
at the Hat Creek Radio Observatory.

282
00:15:38,230 --> 00:15:42,570
现在，我们拥有Allen天线阵列（ATA）
Right now,what we have is the Allen telescope array,

283
00:15:42,570 --> 00:15:46,940
一个建造射电望远镜的 创新手段
an innovative new way to build a radio telescope,

284
00:15:46,940 --> 00:15:51,840
一个由小部件组成的大望远镜
a large telescope out of small pieces.

285
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:53,510
弗里曼：通过将许多小望远镜 联合起来
(Freeman) By linking together

286
00:15:53,510 --> 00:15:56,350
组成一个大的望远镜
a large number of small telescopes,

287
00:15:56,350 --> 00:15:59,650
ATA赋予了天文学家们 一个宽广的视野
the A.T.A. gives astronomers a wide field of view,

288
00:15:59,650 --> 00:16:01,720
相当于
a radial picture

289
00:16:01,720 --> 00:16:05,220
月亮直径10倍大小的辐射图像
10 times the size of the full moon.

290
00:16:05,220 --> 00:16:07,360
现在，我们有42个小天线
Today we've got 42 of these dishes

291
00:16:07,360 --> 00:16:09,130
每个直径20英尺
that are 20 feet across,

292
00:16:09,130 --> 00:16:11,560
我们希望扩展它
and we're looking forward to expanding it

293
00:16:11,560 --> 00:16:15,030
直到总计350个小天线
till there are 350 of these small dishes.

294
00:16:15,030 --> 00:16:17,940
也许，它最终会是正确的工具
It may be just the right tool,finally.

295
00:16:17,940 --> 00:16:21,070
弗里曼： ATA使得SETI能够
(Freeman) The A.T.A. gives SETI the ability

296
00:16:21,070 --> 00:16:25,480
观测宇宙电台的更多频道
to observe many more channels on the cosmic radio dial.

297
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:27,540
Tarter博士：现在的技术条件下
(Dr. Tarter) Now because of technology,

298
00:16:27,550 --> 00:16:29,110
我们同时观测
we simultaneously observe

299
00:16:29,110 --> 00:16:31,550
几亿个频道
hundreds of millions of channels,

300
00:16:31,550 --> 00:16:34,220
在未来，我希望
and I look forward to the future

301
00:16:34,220 --> 00:16:37,420
我能同时观测几十亿个频道
when I can do billions of channels all at once.

302
00:16:37,420 --> 00:16:40,990
科技手段会使搜索工作
So technology has enabled the search

303
00:16:40,990 --> 00:16:44,160
达到足够大的规模
on a scale that might finally be big enough

304
00:16:44,160 --> 00:16:46,630
最终观测到一些东西
to be able to detect something.

305
00:16:46,630 --> 00:16:52,300
弗里曼：SETI的科学家们 已经监听星星达50年之久
(Freeman) SETI scientists have wiretapped the stars for 50 years,

306
00:16:52,300 --> 00:16:56,470
但仍旧没有地外生命的迹象
but there are still no sign of extraterrestrial life.

307
00:16:56,470 --> 00:17:00,940
沉默还在继续
The great silence remains.

308
00:17:00,950 --> 00:17:03,880
那也许不会令人吃惊
And maybe that's not surprising.

309
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:11,660
(Davies) SETI实在是 一个大海捞针的搜索
(Davies) SETI really is a needle-in-the-haystack search

310
00:17:11,660 --> 00:17:13,990
甚至没人能保证 海里真有一根针
without any guarantee that there's a needle out there.

311
00:17:15,860 --> 00:17:17,690
弗里曼：Paul Davies教授
(Freeman) Professor Paul Davies

312
00:17:17,700 --> 00:17:21,060
物理学家兼SETI成员
is a physicist and SETI affiliate.

313
00:17:21,070 --> 00:17:25,470
但他是组织中的异教徒
But he's something of a heretic within the group.

314
00:17:25,470 --> 00:17:27,100
SETI的传统想法是
The traditional approach to SETI

315
00:17:27,110 --> 00:17:29,470
用射电望远镜扫描天空
is to scan the skies with the radio telescope

316
00:17:29,470 --> 00:17:30,770
希望能够接收到
in the hope of picking up

317
00:17:30,780 --> 00:17:32,810
外星人文明向地球有意识 聚焦发射的
a message from some alien civilization

318
00:17:32,810 --> 00:17:35,850
无线电信号
that's been deliberately beamed towards Earth.

319
00:17:35,850 --> 00:17:39,150
我不认为这是可信的 原因如下
I don't think that's credible,and here's why.

320
00:17:40,890 --> 00:17:44,290
整个SETI工作的最大问题在于
One of the big problems in this whole SETI business

321
00:17:44,290 --> 00:17:46,420
忽视了宇宙的巨大规模
is the vast scale of things.

322
00:17:46,420 --> 00:17:48,990
宇宙实在是太大太大了
The universe is really,really big.

323
00:17:48,990 --> 00:17:53,400
以人类的标准 宇宙惊人的巨大
By human standards,it's just stupendously big.

324
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:55,870
想象我们地板上有一粒
Imagine we get the smallest speck of dirt

325
00:17:55,870 --> 00:17:57,170
最小的灰尘
from the desert floor

326
00:17:57,170 --> 00:18:00,740
假设它代表我们的太阳系
and that that represents the size of our solar system

327
00:18:00,740 --> 00:18:02,610
以冥王星的轨道为界
right out to the orbit of Pluto,

328
00:18:02,610 --> 00:18:05,010
那么,"我们银河系有多大?
and then ask,"how big is our galaxy?

329
00:18:05,010 --> 00:18:06,440
"如果你要跑到银河的另一端
"How far would you have to go

330
00:18:06,440 --> 00:18:09,250
需要多远呢？"
to get to the far side of the Milky Way?"

331
00:18:09,250 --> 00:18:11,650
答案是，从这里
And the answer is all the way to those hills

332
00:18:11,650 --> 00:18:14,120
跑到那边的山这么远
right in the distance over there.

333
00:18:14,120 --> 00:18:15,990
如果你问"要到达
And if you ask,"how far do you have to go

334
00:18:15,990 --> 00:18:18,160
我们观察到的宇宙边缘需要多远?"
to get to the edge of the observable universe?"

335
00:18:18,160 --> 00:18:21,060
呃，你需要跑到火星的轨道上去
Well,you'd need to go right up to the orbit of Mars.

336
00:18:25,300 --> 00:18:27,400
弗里曼： 有这样一种说法
(Freeman) It's often said

337
00:18:27,400 --> 00:18:29,370
我们广播和电视信号
that the because our radio and television signals

338
00:18:29,370 --> 00:18:31,700
已经向空间中发射了80年了
have beamed into space for 80 years now,

339
00:18:31,700 --> 00:18:34,870
外星人们应该已经找出 我们在哪里
aliens should have figured out that we're here

340
00:18:34,870 --> 00:18:37,770
并给我们发射信息了
and have sent us a message.

341
00:18:37,780 --> 00:18:42,380
但这个说法华丽的无视了 一个基本的物理定律
But this argument overlooks a basic law of physics.

342
00:18:42,380 --> 00:18:43,980
另一种思路是考虑
Another way of thinking about this is

343
00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:45,250
光的速度
in terms of the speed of light,

344
00:18:45,250 --> 00:18:49,450
光速每秒30万千米
which travels at 186,000 miles every second.

345
00:18:49,450 --> 00:18:53,160
横穿银河系需要 以光速旅行10万年
It takes light about 100,000 years to cross our galaxy,

346
00:18:53,160 --> 00:18:59,100
横穿宇宙则需要137亿年
and it takes 13.7 billion years for it to cross the universe.

347
00:18:59,100 --> 00:19:00,830
我们的第一次 泄漏到宇宙中的广播信号
Our first radio signals,

348
00:19:00,830 --> 00:19:04,230
是在大约100年前
which leaked out into space about 100 years ago,

349
00:19:04,240 --> 00:19:07,270
可以通过比较来说明一下 信号飞了有多远
have gone about that far by comparison.

350
00:19:07,270 --> 00:19:11,640
假设在一千光年外 有一个文明
Suppose there's a civilization 1,000 light-years away,

351
00:19:11,640 --> 00:19:15,880
即使是按照SETI的乐观标准 这也是非常之近了
and that's actually pretty close by even SETI optimist standards.

352
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:18,520
好，他们看不到 今日模样的地球
Well,they don't see Earth as it is today.

353
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:20,780
他们看到的 是1000年前的地球
They see it as it was 1,000 years ago.

354
00:19:20,790 --> 00:19:22,790
那时还没有射电望远镜
There were no radio telescopes here then,

355
00:19:22,790 --> 00:19:24,350
所以，在他们知道我们存在之前
so it would make no sense

356
00:19:24,360 --> 00:19:26,920
他们是不会开始
for them to start sending messages to us

357
00:19:26,930 --> 00:19:29,030
向我们发射信号的
until they knew that we were on the air.

358
00:19:29,030 --> 00:19:31,330
在我们的第一声广播信号 到达之前
And they're not gonna know we're on the air

359
00:19:31,330 --> 00:19:33,600
他们不会知道我们的存在
until our first radio messages reach them,

360
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:35,970
那可要再等上900年
and that's gonna be in another 900 years.

361
00:19:35,970 --> 00:19:40,000
[ 频率调制 ]
[ Frequencies modulating ]

362
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,910
弗里曼： 接听其它星球的信号 是一场持久战
(Freeman) Listening for signals from the stars is a long shot.

363
00:19:43,910 --> 00:19:46,380
我们怎样才能更好的完成
So,how can we do a better job

364
00:19:46,380 --> 00:19:50,150
搜寻地外生命的工作泥？
of tracking down extraterrestrial life?

365
00:19:50,150 --> 00:19:54,220
在SETI监听着宇宙之声时
As SETI monitors its cosmic wiretaps,

366
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:55,750
另一群科学侦探
another set of science detectives

367
00:19:55,750 --> 00:19:57,820
沿着全新的道路前进...
follow radical new leads...

368
00:19:57,820 --> 00:20:02,190
可能会直接将我们 带到ET的家门口
Leads that may take us right to E.T.'s doorstep.

369
00:20:09,790 --> 00:20:12,260
弗里曼： 收听其他星球的信号
(Freeman) Listening for signals from the stars

370
00:20:12,260 --> 00:20:13,390
是搜索宇宙中其他智慧生物的
is one way to search

371
00:20:13,390 --> 00:20:16,730
手段之一
for other intelligent life in the universe.

372
00:20:16,730 --> 00:20:18,700
但还有其他法子
But there are other ways

373
00:20:18,700 --> 00:20:21,100
能够把ET生物揪出来
we can find out where E.T. lives.

374
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,240
天文学家Geoff Marcy 搜寻天空
Astronomer Geoff Marcy hunts the sky

375
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:29,440
寻找能够供养外星生命的行星
for planets that could sustain alien life.

376
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:31,110
很多年来
For many years,

377
00:20:31,110 --> 00:20:34,310
人们认为他可能疯了
people thought he might be crazy.

378
00:20:34,320 --> 00:20:36,280
(Marcy) 当我告诉别的科学家
(Marcy) When I would tell other scientists

379
00:20:36,280 --> 00:20:38,590
我在追寻围绕其它恒星的行星
that I was hunting for planets around other stars,

380
00:20:38,590 --> 00:20:41,150
我会低头研究自己的鞋子
they would look down at their shoes,

381
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:42,790
我不好意思
embarrassed for me.

382
00:20:42,790 --> 00:20:45,830
你可能也会在金字塔里寻找
You might as well be looking for fairies

383
00:20:45,830 --> 00:20:50,860
天外飞仙或者外星文明
or for alien civilizations in the pyramids.

384
00:20:50,870 --> 00:20:54,030
弗里曼：很难令人相信
(Freeman) It's hard to believe,

385
00:20:54,030 --> 00:20:56,100
但是直到近来，还没有证据表明
but until recently,there was no proof

386
00:20:56,100 --> 00:20:59,210
在我们太阳系外存在其它行星
that other planets exist outside our solar system.

387
00:20:59,210 --> 00:21:03,660
地球上的所有望远镜和 太空中的卫星
No telescope on Earth nor satellite in space

388
00:21:03,660 --> 00:21:07,900
它们从主星反射的光太弱了
They don't reflect very much light from their host star.

389
00:21:07,950 --> 00:21:10,050
(Marcy) 原因太简单了
(Marcy) The reason is simple.

390
00:21:10,060 --> 00:21:12,160
行星没有闪光
Planets don't shine.

391
00:21:12,190 --> 00:21:16,960
它们不过是一堆暗尘
They're dark specks of dust,if you will,

392
00:21:16,960 --> 00:21:19,760
如果你在太空中漫游
floating around the universe,

393
00:21:19,870 --> 00:21:21,000
人们知道
and people understood

394
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:23,230
你可能无法探测到它们
you probably could never detect them.

395
00:21:24,640 --> 00:21:27,770
弗里曼：Marcy却不信邪
(Freeman)Marcy refused to accept that.

396
00:21:27,770 --> 00:21:30,010
冒着受到专业级嘲笑的危险
Risking professional ridicule,

397
00:21:30,010 --> 00:21:33,910
他决心找到合适的方法，确定远方世界的位置
he was determined to find a way to spot far-off worlds.

398
00:21:33,910 --> 00:21:37,420
他甚至找到了一个
Eventually, he found one.

399
00:21:37,420 --> 00:21:41,750
事实表明，虽然行星能够躲过我们的眼睛
It turns out that while planets may hide from our eyes,

400
00:21:41,760 --> 00:21:44,720
它们却躲不过所环绕的星星
they can't hide from the stars they orbit,

401
00:21:44,730 --> 00:21:50,030
天文学家称之为主星
what astronomers call their host stars.

402
00:21:50,030 --> 00:21:52,600
找到行星实际上是项简单任务
Finding planets is actually very simple.

403
00:21:52,600 --> 00:21:56,670
我们看着主星，我的脑袋代表主星
We watch the host star,represented by my head,

404
00:21:56,670 --> 00:21:59,770
还有行星，这个网球代表 行星
as the planet,represented by the tennis ball,

405
00:21:59,770 --> 00:22:05,180
行星环绕主星，两者之间有引力作用
orbits that star and pulls gravitationally on the star.

406
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:10,180
于是，主星，我的脑袋 会摇摆不定
As it does so, the star,my head, wobbles around,

407
00:22:10,180 --> 00:22:12,020
这是行星引力的作用
being pulled by the planet,

408
00:22:12,020 --> 00:22:13,490
回到地球
and back on Earth,

409
00:22:13,490 --> 00:22:17,060
我们的望远镜能够观测到 行星环绕造成的
our telescopes can watch to see the wobble of the host star

410
00:22:17,060 --> 00:22:18,590
主星抖动
as the planet goes around it.

411
00:22:18,590 --> 00:22:20,760
即使你看不到行星
Even if you don't see the planet,

412
00:22:20,760 --> 00:22:23,030
你可以看到恒星在抖动
you can see the star wobble around.

413
00:22:23,030 --> 00:22:25,330
很难直接观测
It's difficult to see it directly,

414
00:22:25,330 --> 00:22:28,200
但我们观测星光的多普勒效应
but we use the Doppler shift of the star's light.

415
00:22:28,200 --> 00:22:29,740
当星星接近你
As the star comes at you,

416
00:22:29,740 --> 00:22:33,040
传导地球的光波被压缩
the light waves emitted toward the Earth get compressed,

417
00:22:33,040 --> 00:22:34,940
当星星远离你
and as the star goes away from you,

418
00:22:34,940 --> 00:22:36,780
光波被拉伸
the light waves get stretched out

419
00:22:36,780 --> 00:22:38,940
当它们旅行到地球
in their travels to the Earth.

420
00:22:38,950 --> 00:22:41,210
你就可以观察到
And so you can see the light waves

421
00:22:41,210 --> 00:22:44,080
光波被压缩、拉伸、压缩、拉伸
compressing and stretching and compressing and stretching,

422
00:22:44,080 --> 00:22:47,020
我们人类的眼睛就看到颜色的变化
which, to our human eyes,means the color changes.

423
00:22:47,020 --> 00:22:48,950
我们看到颜色
We see the colors change

424
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:52,260
变得偏蓝、偏红、偏蓝、偏红...
from bluer to redder to bluer to redder,

425
00:22:52,260 --> 00:22:55,390
我们可以在望远镜的后面，量测这种变化
and we can measure that at the back end of a telescope.

426
00:22:55,400 --> 00:23:00,070
这种效应以声波的多普勒效应闻名于世
This effect is well-known,the Doppler effect, with sound.

427
00:23:00,070 --> 00:23:01,270
[ 火车鸣笛 ]
[ Train whistle blows ]

428
00:23:01,270 --> 00:23:03,970
当火车经过你时
You can hear the pitch of a train whistle change

429
00:23:03,970 --> 00:23:05,900
你可以听到汽笛声的变化
as the train goes by you.

430
00:23:07,410 --> 00:23:09,840
即使你双眼紧闭
Even with your eyes closed,

431
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:12,410
你仍可分辨火车是接近还是远离
you can tell whether the train is coming or going,

432
00:23:12,410 --> 00:23:14,750
来自恒星的光波也是如此
and so it is with light waves from a star.

433
00:23:14,750 --> 00:23:17,350
你可以通过光波的变化
You can tell whether the star is coming or going

434
00:23:17,350 --> 00:23:20,020
分辨星星是接近还是远离
from the changing pitch of the light waves.

435
00:23:22,820 --> 00:23:25,420
弗里曼：近10年来
(Freeman) For nearly a decade,

436
00:23:25,420 --> 00:23:27,460
利用多普勒效应方法搜寻行星的工作
the Doppler shift method of planet hunting

437
00:23:27,460 --> 00:23:30,960
仍然是试验性的，还没取得好效果
was an experiment with no positive results.

438
00:23:30,960 --> 00:23:33,900
Marcy仍然不能证明
Marcy still couldn't prove

439
00:23:33,900 --> 00:23:37,700
在我们的太阳系外存在行星
that planets exist outside our solar system.

440
00:23:37,700 --> 00:23:40,040
但是，慢慢的，他赢得了
But he slowly won a few astronomers over

441
00:23:40,040 --> 00:23:42,540
一些天文学家的认同
to his way of thinking.

442
00:23:42,540 --> 00:23:46,310
在探索之路上，他不再是形影相吊了
He was no longer the sole Detective on the investigation.

443
00:23:47,510 --> 00:23:52,650
1995年，他的耐心得到回报
In 1995, his patience was rewarded.

444
00:23:53,350 --> 00:23:56,290
一个瑞士天文学团队
A group of Swiss astronomers had their eyes

445
00:23:56,290 --> 00:23:58,890
在半人马座发现了一个明亮物体
on a bright object in the Pegasus constellation

446
00:23:58,890 --> 00:24:02,490
称为51半人马b
called 51 Pegasi b.

447
00:24:02,490 --> 00:24:03,760
瑞士人认为
The Swiss suspected

448
00:24:03,760 --> 00:24:06,660
他们找到了众人梦寐以求的——
they had found what everyone was looking for --

449
00:24:06,670 --> 00:24:09,570
一个很大的行星
a very large planet,

450
00:24:09,570 --> 00:24:13,240
太阳系外的第一例
the first one seen outside of our solar system.

451
00:24:13,240 --> 00:24:15,670
但他们需要证实
But they needed confirmation.

452
00:24:15,670 --> 00:24:18,580
很幸运，我的学生Paul Butler和我
Luckily, my student, Paul Butler, and I

453
00:24:18,580 --> 00:24:22,410
搞到了Lick观测站
had telescope time assigned to us just a week later

454
00:24:22,410 --> 00:24:25,220
3m望远镜的观测时间
on the Lick Observatory 3-meter telescope

455
00:24:25,220 --> 00:24:27,390
连续4个晚上
four consecutive nights,

456
00:24:27,390 --> 00:24:29,750
真是走运啊
and as luck would have it,

457
00:24:29,760 --> 00:24:33,090
环绕51半人马星的这颗卫星，它可能的公转周期正好是
the supposed orbital period of this planet around 51 peg

458
00:24:33,090 --> 00:24:34,430
4天
was four days.

459
00:24:34,430 --> 00:24:35,730
太符合了
Perfect match.

460
00:24:35,730 --> 00:24:39,000
我们赶到望远镜那儿，4个晚上全是晴天
We went to the telescope. All four nights were clear.

461
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,500
我们测量了51半人马的多普勒光谱
We measured the Doppler shift of 51 peg,

462
00:24:41,500 --> 00:24:44,840
我们百分之百的确信
and we drove off the mountain 100% convinced

463
00:24:44,840 --> 00:24:47,510
瑞士人是正确的
that the Swiss had been correct.

464
00:24:47,510 --> 00:24:49,170
这可真是非凡时刻
It was a marvelous moment,

465
00:24:49,170 --> 00:24:51,880
在95年10月中
and the world was introduced at that time,

466
00:24:51,880 --> 00:24:54,410
这一成果向世界宣布
in mid October '95,

467
00:24:54,410 --> 00:24:57,820
宣布我们的太阳系不是孤独的
to the notion that our solar system was not alone.

468
00:24:59,750 --> 00:25:05,120
弗里曼：51半人马b的发现，改变了整个游戏
(Freeman) The discovery of 51 Pegasi b changed the entire game.

469
00:25:05,120 --> 00:25:07,690
利用多普勒效应寻找行星的方法
The Doppler shift method of planet detection

470
00:25:07,690 --> 00:25:08,990
被证明是有效的
was proven successful,

471
00:25:08,990 --> 00:25:10,560
很快，Marcy和同事们
and soon Marcy and his colleagues

472
00:25:10,560 --> 00:25:13,330
找到了更多的地外世界
found more and more alien worlds.

473
00:25:13,330 --> 00:25:17,440
他们渐渐证实了450个
They have slowly confirmed nearly 450.

474
00:25:17,440 --> 00:25:20,810
而实际好像存在几十亿个
There are likely billions more.

475
00:25:20,810 --> 00:25:23,570
但是Marcy的目标没有改变
But Marcy's goal hasn't changed.

476
00:25:23,580 --> 00:25:26,140
他想找到类地行星
He wants to find worlds like Earth

477
00:25:26,150 --> 00:25:29,210
在适宜的轨道区域，环绕主星运行
that orbit in the habitable zones around their host stars,

478
00:25:29,210 --> 00:25:31,250
舒适的行星
the comfortable place

479
00:25:31,250 --> 00:25:34,590
不是太热也不是太冷...
where it's not too hot and not too cold...

480
00:25:34,590 --> 00:25:38,660
有条件供养生命的世界
Worlds that could possibly support some form of life.

481
00:25:41,890 --> 00:25:43,900
在合适轨道区域的行星
Planets in this habitable zone

482
00:25:43,900 --> 00:25:48,070
可能会有我们所知的，组成生命的要素
may have a key ingredient for life as we know it.

483
00:25:48,070 --> 00:25:51,040
如果你研究一下地球上的生物
If you took any organism on Earth,

484
00:25:51,040 --> 00:25:52,800
如果你提取出水份
and you took out the water,

485
00:25:52,810 --> 00:25:55,510
你会得到氨基酸粉末
you'd end up with some powdered amino acids,

486
00:25:55,510 --> 00:25:59,440
蛋白质，核酸，一些脂肪——
proteins, nucleic acids,a few fats --

487
00:25:59,440 --> 00:26:01,050
这些东西
you know, these sorts of things.

488
00:26:01,050 --> 00:26:04,180
但是，在脱水的状态下，它们不会工作
But they don't work in a powdered form.

489
00:26:04,180 --> 00:26:06,680
它们不能组成生命
You don't get the chemistry that would make life.

490
00:26:06,690 --> 00:26:08,420
所以你需要某种液体
So you need some kind of liquid,

491
00:26:08,420 --> 00:26:12,690
某种溶剂，能够溶解这些化学品
some kind of solvent to dissolve all these chemicals in.

492
00:26:12,690 --> 00:26:16,330
地球上的生命，都选择用水做溶剂
What all life on Earth uses as its solvent is water,

493
00:26:16,330 --> 00:26:18,060
液态水
liquid water,

494
00:26:18,060 --> 00:26:20,930
所以，这是我们寻找地外生命的第一步
and so that is what we look for as a first step

495
00:26:20,930 --> 00:26:24,500
寻找液态水
to looking for life elsewhere -- the presence of liquid water.

496
00:26:26,510 --> 00:26:29,810
弗里曼： 但是找到适合生命的行星不容易
(Freeman) But finding habitable planets isn't easy.

497
00:26:29,810 --> 00:26:32,280
像地球这样的行星很小
Planets like Earth are small,

498
00:26:32,280 --> 00:26:36,080
而且它们的主星要明亮几十亿倍
and their host stars shine billions of times brighter,

499
00:26:36,080 --> 00:26:39,020
很难看到行星
so they're hard to see.

500
00:26:39,020 --> 00:26:43,120
寻找主星摇摆同样不容易
Looking for solar wobbles isn't easy, either.

501
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:46,220
(Marcy)现在，假象一个小点的行星
(Marcy) Now imagine a smaller planet.

502
00:26:46,230 --> 00:26:48,990
我的脑袋仍然代表主星
My head still represents the host star,

503
00:26:48,990 --> 00:26:52,330
这个小行星环绕着主星
and as this small planet orbits the star,

504
00:26:52,330 --> 00:26:54,270
它的质量太小了
it has so little mass,

505
00:26:54,270 --> 00:26:57,970
它很难以引力拉动我的脑袋
it hardly yanks gravitationally on my head,

506
00:26:57,970 --> 00:26:59,840
很难拉动主星
the host star, at all,

507
00:26:59,840 --> 00:27:03,210
探测像地球这么大的小型行星
making the detection of small Earth-sized planets

508
00:27:03,210 --> 00:27:05,410
非常困难
very difficult.

509
00:27:05,410 --> 00:27:08,180
弗里曼： Marcy已经从疯狂的门外
(Freeman) Marcy has gone from crazy outsider

510
00:27:08,180 --> 00:27:12,550
迈向职业的巅峰，一直矢志不渝
to the peak of his profession without changing his position.

511
00:27:12,550 --> 00:27:15,690
挑战面前他从不低头
He doesn't back down in the face of a challenge.

512
00:27:18,320 --> 00:27:22,830
他计划一次扫描宇宙的一个区域
He plans to keep combing the cosmos a section at a time,

513
00:27:22,830 --> 00:27:26,060
寻找和太阳类似的恒星
looking for stars similar to our sun

514
00:27:26,060 --> 00:27:29,700
享受我们现有科技的成果
and enjoying the fruits of our ever-evolving technology.

515
00:27:33,440 --> 00:27:34,910
现在
Here we are,

516
00:27:34,910 --> 00:27:38,040
是回答一个古希腊人提出的问题的时候了
about to answer a question that the ancient Greeks asked

517
00:27:38,040 --> 00:27:41,880
无疑，之前的人们也问过同样的问题
and humans undoubtedly asked even before then.

518
00:27:41,880 --> 00:27:45,280
这是人类历史上的宝贵时刻
It's a treasure of a moment in human history

519
00:27:45,280 --> 00:27:48,550
我们的手边突然有了
that we suddenly have at our fingertips

520
00:27:48,550 --> 00:27:51,860
望远镜，计算机，光传感器
the telescopes, the computers,the light detectors,

521
00:27:51,860 --> 00:27:57,300
以及回答一个哲学问题的知识
and the knowledge to answer a philosophical question

522
00:27:57,300 --> 00:28:00,400
人们从太古就开始追问
that humans have been asking since antiquity.

523
00:28:06,130 --> 00:28:09,500
年轻时，我读了很多科幻小说
As a youngster, I read a lot of science fiction,

524
00:28:09,510 --> 00:28:12,480
打开了我的思想
which opened my mind to the possibility

525
00:28:12,480 --> 00:28:14,280
在其他的世界，可能存在生命
of life on other worlds.

526
00:28:14,280 --> 00:28:18,450
直到近来，我们人类仍然只能猜测这种可能性
Until recently, we humans could only guess about this.

527
00:28:18,450 --> 00:28:24,300
但现在，我们生活在科学蓬勃发展的时期..
But today we live in a scientific renaissance...

528
00:28:24,960 --> 00:28:27,460
...技术的黄金时代
...A golden age of technology

529
00:28:27,460 --> 00:28:29,930
现在，想象的可能性
where fictional possibilities give way

530
00:28:29,930 --> 00:28:33,300
指引了特别的发现
to extraordinary discoveries.

531
00:28:33,300 --> 00:28:36,540
感谢我们的高科技工具
Thanks to our high-tech tools,

532
00:28:36,540 --> 00:28:37,910
我们距离解决外星生命的谜题
we may be on the verge

533
00:28:37,910 --> 00:28:40,280
也许只有一步之遥
of solving the mystery of alien life.

534
00:28:42,350 --> 00:28:45,610
比如，直到最近
For instance, until recently,

535
00:28:45,610 --> 00:28:47,750
追寻ET的调查员们
the investigators tracking down E.T.

536
00:28:47,750 --> 00:28:50,150
还不得不在地球上，开展搜索工作
had to search for him down here on Earth.

537
00:28:50,150 --> 00:28:56,520
而现在，我们终于可以在外太空开展搜索了
But now we finally have a Detective in outer space.

538
00:29:00,660 --> 00:29:03,200
(男声) 发射，我们发射了
(Man) Liftoff. We have liftoff.

539
00:29:03,200 --> 00:29:07,240
弗里曼： 在2009年3月6日
(Freeman) On march 6, 2009,

540
00:29:07,240 --> 00:29:11,140
NASA发射了开普勒太空望远镜
NASA launched the Kepler Space Telescope.

541
00:29:11,140 --> 00:29:13,240
开普勒望远镜是第一个
Kepler is the first-ever satellite

542
00:29:13,240 --> 00:29:15,010
致力于寻找太阳系外行星的
solely devoted to the hunt

543
00:29:15,010 --> 00:29:17,580
探测器
for planets outside our solar system.

544
00:29:17,580 --> 00:29:18,950
[ 哔 ]
[ Beeping ]

545
00:29:18,950 --> 00:29:22,150
人们的愿望是，开普勒不仅找到更多的行星
The hope is that Kepler will not just find more planets,

546
00:29:22,150 --> 00:29:23,620
而且还能找到
but will discover planets

547
00:29:23,620 --> 00:29:28,190
体积和大气条件类似地球的行星
roughly similar in size and atmosphere to Earth.

548
00:29:28,190 --> 00:29:32,560
这样的行星能够养活生命
Such planets could support life.

549
00:29:32,560 --> 00:29:35,830
William Borucki是开普勒计划的
William Borucki is the principal investigator

550
00:29:35,830 --> 00:29:37,330
负责人
for the Kepler mission.

551
00:29:39,770 --> 00:29:44,040
他为这项工作计划了25年
He's been planning this for 25 years.

552
00:29:44,040 --> 00:29:45,900
当我还是个小孩
Ever since I was a little boy,

553
00:29:45,910 --> 00:29:47,710
我对空间探索很敢兴趣
I was interested in space exploration.

554
00:29:47,710 --> 00:29:50,980
我们会躺在车库顶上看流星
We used to lie on a garage roof during meteor showers

555
00:29:50,980 --> 00:29:54,320
用相机拍下流星的照片
and use cameras to take pictures of meteors.

556
00:29:54,320 --> 00:29:56,680
所以为NASA工作可算得上美梦成真
So it's a dream come true to work with NASA

557
00:29:56,690 --> 00:29:59,190
特别是能够参与一项
and actually be able to come up with a mission

558
00:29:59,190 --> 00:30:02,090
帮助我们理解外太空的计划
that will help us understand what might be out in space.

559
00:30:02,090 --> 00:30:06,190
弗里曼：开普勒的美在于简洁
(Freeman) The beauty of Kepler is its simplicity.

560
00:30:06,190 --> 00:30:07,430
它通过测量
It looks for planets

561
00:30:07,430 --> 00:30:10,030
行星从它的主星前飞过时
by measuring how much light a planet blocks

562
00:30:10,030 --> 00:30:12,330
遮挡的光线量来寻找行星
when it passes in front of its sun.

563
00:30:12,330 --> 00:30:16,900
这被称为经过效应
This is called a transit.

564
00:30:16,910 --> 00:30:21,480
一个熟悉的例子是，在月食或者日食时
A familiar example occurs during a lunar or a solar eclipse

565
00:30:21,480 --> 00:30:25,910
阳光被月亮或者地球所遮挡
when sunlight is blocked by the moon or Earth's shadow.

566
00:30:25,910 --> 00:30:29,450
我们肉眼即可观察到这些现象
This is easy for us to see with the naked eye.

567
00:30:29,450 --> 00:30:33,620
但是要找到一个类地行星，经过一个遥远的恒星
But finding an Earthlike planet transiting a distant star

568
00:30:33,620 --> 00:30:36,590
那可是困难的多
is much more difficult.

569
00:30:36,590 --> 00:30:39,190
一个地球尺度的经过效应，可是非常非常小
An Earth-size transit is really, really tiny.

570
00:30:39,190 --> 00:30:43,430
这有点像观察一个跳蚤，穿过汽车头灯
So it's like watching a flea crossing a car headlight,

571
00:30:43,430 --> 00:30:45,330
很长的一段距离
a really long distance.

572
00:30:45,330 --> 00:30:47,300
你找到它，测量它
And you find that, and you measure it,

573
00:30:47,300 --> 00:30:48,770
精确的测量
and you measure it accurately.

574
00:30:48,770 --> 00:30:53,110
弗里曼：开普勒测量光线的这些微小变化
(Freeman) Kepler measures these minute changes in light.

575
00:30:53,110 --> 00:30:55,780
在光与暗之间的微小差异
Tiny differences between light and dark

576
00:30:55,780 --> 00:30:58,650
指示出行星在哪里
tells it where the planets are.

577
00:30:58,650 --> 00:31:01,880
你所见的，是一条曲线，表面光线是常量
So what you see is a curve showing light being constant,

578
00:31:01,880 --> 00:31:04,550
一个波动，行星经过了，它又恢复了
a dip, the planet goes across, it comes back up again.

579
00:31:04,550 --> 00:31:07,050
你观察每个恒星
So you're looking for that dip in light

580
00:31:07,060 --> 00:31:09,590
找寻行星经过时产生的波动
for each star when a planet goes across,

581
00:31:09,590 --> 00:31:12,230
行星越大，遮挡越多的光线
and the bigger the planet, the more light it blocks,

582
00:31:12,230 --> 00:31:13,490
波动越大
the bigger the dip.

583
00:31:13,500 --> 00:31:15,530
所以我们可以通过波动大小
So we can tell the size of the planet

584
00:31:15,530 --> 00:31:17,030
判断行星的体积
from the size of the dip.

585
00:31:17,030 --> 00:31:22,540
弗里曼： 但是，实际上不能一次观察一个恒星
(Freeman) But it isn't practical to observe one star at a time.

586
00:31:22,540 --> 00:31:26,970
Borucki需要找到同时观察多个恒星的办法
Borucki had to find a way to look at many stars together,

587
00:31:26,980 --> 00:31:31,480
这是一个空前的、被人称为不可能的目标
an unprecedented, some said impossible, goal.

588
00:31:31,480 --> 00:31:33,280
(Borucki)我们必须表明
(Borucki) We had to show

589
00:31:33,280 --> 00:31:36,650
我们可以测量这些星星的亮度
that we can measure the brightness of these stars,

590
00:31:36,650 --> 00:31:39,990
同时测量10万个星体
100,000 of them simultaneously.

591
00:31:39,990 --> 00:31:42,560
这招来了大量的怀疑
It was met with a great deal of skepticism,

592
00:31:42,560 --> 00:31:44,560
在科学界
and the science community

593
00:31:44,560 --> 00:31:47,400
有文章声称，"这是不可能的"
actually published articles saying, "that can't be done."

594
00:31:47,400 --> 00:31:49,460
所以这真的费了我们很大事
And so it took us quite a while

595
00:31:49,460 --> 00:31:51,530
我们表明，是的，你可以做到
to show that, yes, you can do that.

596
00:31:51,530 --> 00:31:55,100
你可以建造一个大视野的望远镜
You can build a wide field of your telescope

597
00:31:55,100 --> 00:31:56,540
图像像素数很高
with a huge number of pixels

598
00:31:56,540 --> 00:31:58,810
同时观测所有的星体
that measure all these stars simultaneously,

599
00:31:58,810 --> 00:32:01,540
然后，你可以单独测量每一个星球
and then you can watch each and every individual star

600
00:32:01,540 --> 00:32:03,480
看看是什么样的行星在环绕它
to see what planet is crossing it.

601
00:32:05,480 --> 00:32:08,620
弗里曼： 开普勒精度惊人
(Freeman) Kepler does this with amazing accuracy.

602
00:32:08,620 --> 00:32:10,220
[ 快门声 ]
[ Camera shutter clicks ]

603
00:32:10,220 --> 00:32:13,420
(Borucki)实际上，它是一架在太空中运动的巨大照相机
(Borucki) Basically, it's a huge camera that orbits in space.

604
00:32:13,420 --> 00:32:16,060
它不是环绕地球，它在环绕太阳
It does not orbit the Earth. It orbits the sun.

605
00:32:16,060 --> 00:32:18,730
于是它可以观察
And so it can look at one group of stars

606
00:32:18,730 --> 00:32:22,160
天鹅座和天琴座之间的一组恒星
between the Cygnus constellation and the constellation of Lyra,

607
00:32:22,160 --> 00:32:25,400
同时观察15万颗星
150,000 stars simultaneously,

608
00:32:25,400 --> 00:32:28,340
每6秒钟测量一个恒星
and make a measurement of each of those every 6 seconds.

609
00:32:28,340 --> 00:32:31,570
弗里曼：一旦开普勒探测到行星
(Freeman) Once Kepler detects a planet,

610
00:32:31,570 --> 00:32:35,010
会结合行星质量和表面温度
the planet's orbital size can be calculated

611
00:32:35,010 --> 00:32:38,480
计算行星的轨道尺寸
along with its mass and surface temperature.

612
00:32:38,480 --> 00:32:41,480
作为开普勒科学工作组的一员
As a member of the Kepler science working group,

613
00:32:41,480 --> 00:32:44,320
Geoff Marcy相信，开普勒是寻找地外生命的
Geoff Marcy believes that Kepler is the next great step

614
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:47,520
伟大一步
towards finding life on other planets.

615
00:32:51,230 --> 00:32:53,330
(Marcy)我认为在接下来的几年
(Marcy) I think in the next few years,

616
00:32:53,330 --> 00:32:56,330
我们会找到第一颗和地球尺寸、质量差不多
we will find the first planets of Earth size, Earth mass,

617
00:32:56,330 --> 00:32:58,530
甚至连温度也相似的星球
maybe even Earthlike temperatures,

618
00:32:58,530 --> 00:33:02,100
意味着它适合生物居住
rendering them habitable.

619
00:33:02,100 --> 00:33:04,000
我觉得，公平的说
And I think it's fair to say

620
00:33:04,010 --> 00:33:07,410
接下来的一二十年中，最主要的目标是
that one of the great goals of the next decade or two

621
00:33:07,410 --> 00:33:09,910
建造一个能够探测行星陆地的装置
is to build a terrestrial planet-finder

622
00:33:09,910 --> 00:33:12,850
能够真正的拍下其它行星的照片
that can actually take pictures of other Earths

623
00:33:12,850 --> 00:33:17,750
确定那个行星是否适合居住
and ascertain whether there's any habitability possible

624
00:33:17,750 --> 00:33:20,960
是否存在生命
on that planet and, indeed, life there, as well.

625
00:33:23,990 --> 00:33:26,990
报酬就是搞清楚
Payoff is just the pure knowledge

626
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:29,260
宇宙中是否存在其它的地球，很多的地球
of are there Earths, lots of Earths out there?

627
00:33:29,260 --> 00:33:33,100
如果有，宇宙中就有可能有大量生命
If there are, there's probably a lot of life out there.

628
00:33:33,100 --> 00:33:36,140
如果事实相反，我们没找到
If the opposite occurs, we don't find any,

629
00:33:36,140 --> 00:33:38,840
那就不会有"星际迷航"了
there never will be a "Star Trek."

630
00:33:38,840 --> 00:33:40,810
我们无处可去
There's no place to go to.

631
00:33:40,810 --> 00:33:43,680
弗里曼： 更强的科技手段
(Freeman) Better technology

632
00:33:43,680 --> 00:33:46,350
使我们更有可能找到其他的地球
is getting us closer to finding those other Earths.

633
00:33:46,350 --> 00:33:50,680
举例来说，通过观测设备
For instance, by fine-tuning our senses,

634
00:33:50,690 --> 00:33:53,390
我们现在能够看到
we can now read the light reflected

635
00:33:53,390 --> 00:33:55,090
行星表面反射的光
off a planet's surface.

636
00:33:55,090 --> 00:33:56,720
这使我们能够判断
This lets us determine

637
00:33:56,730 --> 00:34:00,530
其它行星大气的化学组分
the chemical composition of that planet's atmosphere.

638
00:34:00,530 --> 00:34:04,330
我们现在可以分析大气
Now that we can analyze atmospheres,

639
00:34:04,330 --> 00:34:08,100
我们可以开始寻找地外文明的
we can start looking for the unique environmental signatures

640
00:34:08,100 --> 00:34:11,170
独一无二的环境印记
of alien civilizations.

641
00:34:11,170 --> 00:34:15,580
每种形式的科技，都会在环境中留下足迹
Every form of technology leaves a footprint on its environment.

642
00:34:15,580 --> 00:34:18,250
例如，如果你遥望地球
For example, if you're looking at Earth from a long way away,

643
00:34:18,250 --> 00:34:19,810
你会看到全球变暖
you see global warming.

644
00:34:19,820 --> 00:34:21,280
这就是我们的足迹
That's our footprint.

645
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:24,520
我们可以想象一个文明
Well, in -- we can imagine a civilization

646
00:34:24,520 --> 00:34:27,050
延续了极长的时间
that might have been around for an immense period of time

647
00:34:27,060 --> 00:34:28,760
留下大量的显著印记
would leave a much bigger footprint,

648
00:34:28,760 --> 00:34:30,160
也许不仅是在它的本土星球
maybe not just on its planet,

649
00:34:30,160 --> 00:34:32,290
而是在它整个航天能力所及的环境
but on its entire astronomical environment.

650
00:34:32,290 --> 00:34:35,530
所以我们要搜寻空间中的所有痕迹
So we should look for anything out there in space,

651
00:34:35,530 --> 00:34:37,530
任何的异常
any anomaly, anything that looks like

652
00:34:37,530 --> 00:34:39,630
任何看上去可能不是自然产物的东西
it could not have a natural explanation.

653
00:34:45,170 --> 00:34:48,180
弗里曼： 我们每天寻找新的行星
(Freeman) We're finding new planets every day,

654
00:34:48,180 --> 00:34:52,880
但到目前为止，我们的调查没有发现外星人
but so far, our investigation hasn't turned up any aliens.

655
00:34:52,880 --> 00:34:54,520
如果我们是在错误的地点
What if we're looking in the wrong places

656
00:34:54,520 --> 00:34:55,720
寻找错误的东西怎么办？
for the wrong things?

657
00:34:55,720 --> 00:34:59,420
如果一个真正的外星生物
What if a real-life alien isn't anything like

658
00:34:59,420 --> 00:35:01,690
根本不是我们想象的样子？
the creatures of our imagination?

659
00:35:01,690 --> 00:35:03,120
[ 咆哮 ]
[ Growling ]

660
00:35:12,030 --> 00:35:16,230
我们寻找类地行星和类地生物
We're hunting for Earthlike planets and Earthlike life,

661
00:35:16,230 --> 00:35:21,300
我们还希望找到外星文明
and maybe, we hope, alien civilizations.

662
00:35:21,300 --> 00:35:25,940
但如果它们根本不是类地的，又怎么样？
But what if they aren't Earthlike at all?

663
00:35:25,940 --> 00:35:30,450
如果外星生物根本不需要诸如身体的东西？
What if the aliens don't need things like bodies?

664
00:35:30,450 --> 00:35:34,280
寻找我们所知形式的生命，可能是一个错误
Looking for life as we know it could be a mistake

665
00:35:34,280 --> 00:35:38,050
因为有些人说，我们所知的生命形式
because, some say, life as we know it

666
00:35:38,050 --> 00:35:40,720
很快会成为过时的产物
will soon be an artifact of the past.

667
00:35:44,430 --> 00:35:46,260
(Wright)我认为，我们很有可能
(Wright) I think it's entirely possible

668
00:35:46,260 --> 00:35:48,030
在某些地外行星
that we will discover microbial life

669
00:35:48,030 --> 00:35:49,500
找到微生物
on some extrasolar planet.

670
00:35:49,500 --> 00:35:53,440
但我认为人们真正想要的，是外星人
But I think what people really want is they want aliens.

671
00:35:53,440 --> 00:35:55,240
[ 外星语言交谈 ]
[ Conversing in alien language ]

672
00:35:55,240 --> 00:35:56,970
他们想要能够交谈
They want somebody we can talk to,

673
00:35:56,970 --> 00:35:58,910
具有个体思维能力的外星人
somebody that we can relate to as thinking individuals.

674
00:36:02,350 --> 00:36:04,610
弗里曼：Will Wright是两款革命性电视游戏的
(Freeman) Will Wright is the creator

675
00:36:04,620 --> 00:36:06,820
创造者
of two revolutionary video games --

676
00:36:06,820 --> 00:36:09,030
"模拟人生" 和 "孢子"
"The Sims" and "Spore."

677
00:36:09,030 --> 00:36:13,030
Wright设计创造外星生命的软件
Wright designs software that creates alien life,

678
00:36:13,030 --> 00:36:16,800
这些外星生命，适应宇宙中可能遇到的各种生存条件
creatures uniquely adapted to the myriad conditions

679
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:21,070
进化的独一无二
that might be encountered out of the universe.

680
00:36:21,070 --> 00:36:26,310
当然，这些都是电脑模拟
Of course, it's all simulated inside this computer.

681
00:36:26,310 --> 00:36:28,550
Wright相信，地球上的生命
Wright believes that life on Earth

682
00:36:28,550 --> 00:36:30,050
在激进的变化中
is radically changing.

683
00:36:30,050 --> 00:36:33,220
一种新形式的生命正在被创造
A new form of life is being created --

684
00:36:33,220 --> 00:36:35,450
半人半机械
part human, part machine.

685
00:36:37,990 --> 00:36:40,930
你可以把大部分技术手段
You can almost look at any technology that we use today

686
00:36:40,930 --> 00:36:42,490
看作人类身体的延伸
as an extension of the human body.

687
00:36:42,490 --> 00:36:46,330
建筑物是我们皮肤的延伸
The buildings are an extension of our skin.

688
00:36:46,330 --> 00:36:49,370
汽车是腿的延伸
Cars are an extension of our legs.

689
00:36:49,370 --> 00:36:52,570
电话是嘴的延伸
Telephone is an extension of our mouth.

690
00:36:52,570 --> 00:36:54,040
几千年来
Humans have been so intertwined with technology

691
00:36:54,040 --> 00:36:56,570
人类已经与技术纠结不清
for thousands of years already

692
00:36:56,580 --> 00:36:58,980
要分清这两部分实在太难了
that it's really hard to almost pull the two apart,

693
00:36:58,980 --> 00:37:00,780
看上去只会随着时间
and that seems to just be increasing

694
00:37:00,780 --> 00:37:02,780
不断的增加和加速
and accelerating over time.

695
00:37:02,780 --> 00:37:04,680
弗里曼： 有些人相信
(Freeman) Some believe

696
00:37:04,680 --> 00:37:07,950
我们甚至会彻底抛弃身体
that we might eventually get rid of our bodies completely

697
00:37:07,950 --> 00:37:10,260
变成纯粹的意识生物
and become creatures of pure consciousness,

698
00:37:10,260 --> 00:37:14,390
生活在巨大的计算机中
living in a giant computer.

699
00:37:14,390 --> 00:37:18,400
如果这正在渐渐发生在人类身上
If this is slowly happening to the human race right now,

700
00:37:18,400 --> 00:37:22,930
在遥远的星球上，这一过程是不是已经完成？
has it already happened out in the stars?

701
00:37:22,940 --> 00:37:25,770
(Wright) 如果我们真的在外太空遇到智慧外星生物
(Wright) It's probably likely that if we ever do meet

702
00:37:25,770 --> 00:37:27,970
很有可能
intelligent alien life out there,

703
00:37:27,970 --> 00:37:29,470
我认为
I would imagine

704
00:37:29,480 --> 00:37:33,910
他们已经完成了某种生物转换
that they probably are gonna be transbiological in some sense.

705
00:37:33,910 --> 00:37:35,950
它们可能会是彻底的机器文明
They might be entirely a mechanical civilization.

706
00:37:35,950 --> 00:37:39,450
弗里曼：物理学家Paul Davies同意这一观点
(Freeman) Physicist Paul Davies agrees.

707
00:37:39,450 --> 00:37:40,750
以我的看法
In my view,

708
00:37:40,750 --> 00:37:43,620
生物智能只是宇宙中智能进化的
biological intelligence is just a transitory phase

709
00:37:43,620 --> 00:37:46,120
一个暂时状态
in the evolution of intelligence in the universe.

710
00:37:46,130 --> 00:37:48,660
在几百万年后，你可能会被遍布
So after millions of years, you'll be dealing with something

711
00:37:48,660 --> 00:37:51,330
整个星球的某种东西支配
that might be distributed across the whole surface of a planet.

712
00:37:51,330 --> 00:37:53,060
那不是一个活着的有机体
It wouldn't be a living organism,

713
00:37:53,070 --> 00:37:55,770
而是某种巨大的、悸动的超级大脑
but it'd be a sort of gigantic, throbbing megabrain.

714
00:37:57,000 --> 00:38:00,170
弗里曼： 这些外星超级生命
(Freeman) It's possible these alien super beings,

715
00:38:00,170 --> 00:38:02,540
无论它们是什么
whatever they may be,

716
00:38:02,540 --> 00:38:04,780
很有可能正向我们发射信息
are sending us messages right now,

717
00:38:04,780 --> 00:38:07,580
但我们还不够发达，无法探测到
but we aren't advanced enough to detect them.

718
00:38:07,580 --> 00:38:13,350
那么，又一次，也许这是最好的
Then again, maybe that's for the best.

719
00:38:13,350 --> 00:38:16,190
(Wright)也许这些外星智能
(Wright) It could be that these alien intelligences

720
00:38:16,190 --> 00:38:17,960
拥有完全不同的行为意向
have entirely different intentions,

721
00:38:17,960 --> 00:38:19,190
而且，因此
and, therefore,

722
00:38:19,190 --> 00:38:20,960
也许它们不是在居住地发射广播
maybe they aren't broadcasting where they are.

723
00:38:20,960 --> 00:38:23,500
也许那是一个不友善的宇宙
Maybe it's a hostile universe.

724
00:38:23,500 --> 00:38:25,260
在很多科幻场景中
There are a lot of science-fiction scenarios

725
00:38:25,270 --> 00:38:26,700
小朋友们被召唤进入
that go down the path of

726
00:38:26,700 --> 00:38:28,600
密林深处
the ones that basically start calling out into the deep forest

727
00:38:28,600 --> 00:38:30,570
然后被群狼吞噬
are the ones that get eaten by the wolves.

728
00:38:32,940 --> 00:38:34,170
那些不断试图
The intelligences

729
00:38:34,170 --> 00:38:35,940
和其它文明交流的智慧生物
that are out there trying to talk to everybody else

730
00:38:35,940 --> 00:38:38,030
将是最早被吃掉的
are the ones that get eaten first.

731
00:38:38,030 --> 00:38:40,880
这就是为什么别人都保持沉默
And that's why everybody else is being really quiet.

732
00:38:40,880 --> 00:38:44,620
弗里曼：也许外星人并不友好
(Freeman) Maybe the aliens aren't friendly.

733
00:38:44,620 --> 00:38:47,220
另一方面
On the other hand,

734
00:38:47,220 --> 00:38:50,960
它们也许认为，我们根本不值得进行交流...
they may not even think we're worth contacting...

735
00:38:50,960 --> 00:38:54,960
特别是如果它们真的是古老的悸动的巨大大脑
Particularly if they're ancient throbbing megabrains.

736
00:38:54,960 --> 00:38:58,530
我认为某种经过了几百万年设计的超级智力
I think it's very unlikely that some superintellect

737
00:38:58,530 --> 00:39:02,270
不太可能会有兴趣
that's been the product of millions of years of design

738
00:39:02,270 --> 00:39:04,370
在宇宙之中
is going to have very much interest

739
00:39:04,370 --> 00:39:06,410
游来荡去
in traveling around the universe.

740
00:39:06,410 --> 00:39:08,710
它应该早就派出了探测器
It would long ago have sent probes out

741
00:39:08,710 --> 00:39:11,080
搜集到了所需的信息
and gathered all the information it needed.

742
00:39:11,080 --> 00:39:13,880
我认为更有可能的是
I think it's much more likely that something

743
00:39:13,880 --> 00:39:16,580
经过如此长时间进化
that has been around for such a long period of time

744
00:39:16,580 --> 00:39:18,790
拥有如此强大智能的生物
and has such enormous intellectual power

745
00:39:18,790 --> 00:39:21,990
将会进入某种内赛博空间,
is gonna retreat into a sort of inner cyberspace,

746
00:39:21,990 --> 00:39:26,060
也许对身处的环境已失去兴趣
probably lose interest with its immediate surroundings.

747
00:39:26,060 --> 00:39:28,660
只要有人付电费
So long as someone's paying the electricity bill

748
00:39:28,660 --> 00:39:30,830
并且保卫这个超级大脑
and defending this throbbing megabrain

749
00:39:30,830 --> 00:39:33,730
使其免受小行星撞击和其他威胁
from asteroid impacts and other dangers,

750
00:39:33,730 --> 00:39:37,740
我想它是不会很好动的
I imagine that it's not gonna be very mobile.

751
00:39:37,740 --> 00:39:41,980
弗里曼： 还有另外一个令人沮丧的可能性
(Freeman) And there's another even bleaker possibility.

752
00:39:41,980 --> 00:39:45,380
如果在宇宙中存在
What if there was other intelligent life

753
00:39:45,380 --> 00:39:47,050
智慧生物
in the universe,

754
00:39:47,050 --> 00:39:49,820
但已经消亡了，怎么办？
but now it's gone?

755
00:39:49,820 --> 00:39:51,790
SETI已经搜索了大约50年
SETI's been going for about 50 years now,

756
00:39:51,790 --> 00:39:54,550
所有的天文学家，都只发现了沉默
and all the astronomers have got to show for it is a silence.

757
00:39:54,560 --> 00:39:56,320
我认为沉默是怪诞的
I would say an eerie silence,

758
00:39:56,320 --> 00:39:57,590
因为很多人认为
because many people feel

759
00:39:57,590 --> 00:40:00,130
宇宙中应该有智慧生物
that there should be intelligent life out there,

760
00:40:00,130 --> 00:40:02,100
应该有其他的文明
there should be other civilizations,

761
00:40:02,100 --> 00:40:05,200
如果是这样，这就是不祥的沉寂
and if so, they're ominously quiet.

762
00:40:05,200 --> 00:40:07,400
也许他们保持沉默的原因是
Maybe the reason they're ominously quiet

763
00:40:07,400 --> 00:40:09,040
是因为它们都死了
is because they've all died out.

764
00:40:09,040 --> 00:40:11,400
它们已经自己消亡了
They've wiped themselves out,

765
00:40:11,410 --> 00:40:14,510
或者某种可怕的命运降临
or some horrible fate has befallen them.

766
00:40:14,510 --> 00:40:15,940
如果它们都沉默了
If it is all silence,

767
00:40:15,940 --> 00:40:19,150
那预示着人类的未来
then that bodes ill for the future of humanity.

768
00:40:21,680 --> 00:40:24,650
弗里曼： 所有这些将我们带回起点
(Freeman) All of this leads back to where we started,

769
00:40:24,650 --> 00:40:26,950
一个简单的问题
with a simple question.

770
00:40:26,950 --> 00:40:31,220
宇宙中是否有其他生命
Is there other life in the universe?

771
00:40:31,230 --> 00:40:32,430
必然的
Absolutely.

772
00:40:32,430 --> 00:40:35,830
宇宙中必然有其他的生命形式
There must be other life-forms in the universe,

773
00:40:35,830 --> 00:40:38,530
我甚至愿意更近一步说
and I'm even willing to go the next step and say

774
00:40:38,530 --> 00:40:41,870
必然存在有智慧有技术的生命
there must be intelligent technological life

775
00:40:41,870 --> 00:40:43,470
就在宇宙中的某处
elsewhere in the universe.

776
00:40:43,470 --> 00:40:46,610
当你清点宇宙中的星球
When you count up all the stars that are out there,

777
00:40:46,610 --> 00:40:49,880
这些几十亿、几万亿，甚至更多的类地行星
those billions, trillions, even more Earthlike planets

778
00:40:49,880 --> 00:40:53,480
给予了大量的机会
offer an enormous number of throws of the dice.

779
00:40:53,480 --> 00:40:57,120
及时生命只是百万分之一，甚至十亿分之一的可能
Even if life is one in a million or one in a billion,

780
00:40:57,120 --> 00:41:00,650
机会也实在是太多了
there are just too many throws of the biological dice

781
00:41:00,650 --> 00:41:02,860
在宇宙中
out there in the cosmos

782
00:41:02,860 --> 00:41:05,790
我们孤独存在的可能性不大
for us to be alone.

783
00:41:05,790 --> 00:41:08,330
我们没有证据表明
We have no evidence one way or the other

784
00:41:08,330 --> 00:41:11,870
除了地球不存在智慧生物
of any life beyond Earth, let alone intelligent life.

785
00:41:11,870 --> 00:41:15,940
因此，我的想法是等等看
Therefore, my feeling about it is we wait and see.

786
00:41:17,140 --> 00:41:18,440
在找到证据之前
I've got to be skeptical

787
00:41:18,440 --> 00:41:20,440
我是个怀疑论者
until I get some evidence otherwise.

788
00:41:20,440 --> 00:41:22,110
(女孩) 那是什么，爸?
[ Chickens clucking ] (Girl) What's that, pa?

789
00:41:22,110 --> 00:41:23,840
(男人)什么玩意儿这么吵？
(Man) What's all the racket?

790
00:41:25,310 --> 00:41:29,850
(Wright)我发现如果答案是不，那实在是难以理解
(Wright) I would find it incomprehensible that the answer would be no.

791
00:41:29,850 --> 00:41:31,180
如果答案是不
If it was no,

792
00:41:31,190 --> 00:41:34,420
那可会是很惊人的信息
that would be an amazing bit of information to have.

793
00:41:34,420 --> 00:41:38,220
我的意思是，即使知道我们是多么罕见
I mean, even understanding how rare we are, you know,

794
00:41:38,230 --> 00:41:41,590
也许那些距离非常遥远的
that maybe intelligent life like we might understand it

795
00:41:41,600 --> 00:41:43,700
像我们的智慧生物可能也懂得
is very, very far away from us

796
00:41:43,700 --> 00:41:47,230
这赋予了我们一个极大的责任
puts an incredible responsibility on us.

797
00:41:47,230 --> 00:41:52,670
所有的突发事件、地球、人类、被控制的智力
All the sudden, Earth, humans, directed intelligence

798
00:41:52,670 --> 00:41:54,770
变的极其的宝贵
becomes incredibly precious.

799
00:41:57,380 --> 00:41:59,280
弗里曼： 我们搜寻ET的工作
(Freeman) Our search for E.T.

800
00:41:59,280 --> 00:42:01,920
已经持续了半个世纪
has been going on for half a century.

801
00:42:01,920 --> 00:42:05,520
但是宇宙是个很大的空间
But the universe is a very big place,

802
00:42:05,520 --> 00:42:09,120
要解下它的神秘面纱，我们只是刚上路
and we've only just started to unravel its mysteries.

803
00:42:09,120 --> 00:42:13,190
Tarter博士：如果你在海洋里舀一杯水
(Dr. Tarter) If you dip a glass in the ocean, and you look at it,

804
00:42:13,190 --> 00:42:17,400
里面没有鱼，你会做出何种结论？
and your glass has no fish, what's your conclusion?

805
00:42:17,400 --> 00:42:18,600
你的结论会是
Is your conclusion

806
00:42:18,600 --> 00:42:20,900
海洋里没有鱼吗？
that the ocean doesn't have any fish in it?

807
00:42:20,900 --> 00:42:24,800
或者你的结论是"这是个超大的海
Or is your conclusion, "that's an awfully big ocean,

808
00:42:24,810 --> 00:42:27,740
我并没有舀到足够的水"
and I didn't sample very much of it with my glass?"

809
00:42:27,740 --> 00:42:33,380
对宇宙海洋来说，50年的探索
50 years of exploration of the cosmic oceans

810
00:42:33,380 --> 00:42:35,820
只是毛毛雨
is miniscule.

811
00:42:35,820 --> 00:42:38,750
我们还没有开始看，我们只是刚刚开始搜索
We haven't looked yet. We've hardly begun to search.

812
00:42:38,750 --> 00:42:41,350
在得出任何结论之前
We ought to do a much better job of searching

813
00:42:41,360 --> 00:42:45,190
我们需要完成更好的工作
before we draw any extraordinary conclusions.

814
00:42:51,900 --> 00:42:54,370
弗里曼：生命原料
(Freeman) The building blocks of life

815
00:42:54,370 --> 00:42:56,800
在宇宙中传播
are spread all around the universe.

816
00:42:56,800 --> 00:42:58,640
很难想象它们没有在其中一个行星中上
It's hard to imagine they haven't taken root

817
00:42:58,640 --> 00:43:02,610
生根发芽，开枝散叶
in one of the countless other planets out there.

818
00:43:02,610 --> 00:43:04,080
会有我们认为的
Is any of that life

819
00:43:04,080 --> 00:43:06,780
智慧生物吗?
what we would consider intelligent?

820
00:43:06,780 --> 00:43:10,650
如果外星文明存在
And if alien civilizations are out there,

821
00:43:10,650 --> 00:43:13,520
它们又为什么如此安静？
why are they so quiet?

822
00:43:13,520 --> 00:43:16,620
也许它们的信号还在路上
Maybe their signals are still on the way,

823
00:43:16,620 --> 00:43:19,530
也许它们使用我们无法理解的科技
or maybe they use technology we don't understand,

824
00:43:19,530 --> 00:43:22,400
又或者...
or...

825
00:43:22,400 --> 00:43:25,270
它们不存在
They may not be there at all.

826
00:43:25,270 --> 00:43:27,630
我们一无所知
We just don't know.

827
00:43:27,640 --> 00:43:31,100
但是一件事是肯定的
But one thing is certain.

828
00:43:31,110 --> 00:43:35,140
如果我们找到地外生命
If we find life outside of Earth,

829
00:43:35,140 --> 00:43:37,020
这会深刻的改变
it will profoundly change

830
00:43:37,030 --> 00:43:40,330
我们看待生命和自身的方式
the way we look at life and ourselves.

831
00:43:41,240 --> 00:43:45,140
就在此时，我们拥有希望和梦想...
In the meantime, we have our hopes and dreams...

832
00:43:46,150 --> 00:43:48,250
还有宇宙的沉默
And the silence of the cosmos.

833
00:43:48,760 --> 00:43:52,780
-- 感谢观赏 --

